# Webview (Last 100)

- Table columns: TIME, MODEL, COST, TOKENS, INPUT / OUTPUT, KIND. Newest first. Monospace body.
- Six tabs: **Last N Cursor queries** (default 1000), **Statistics**, **Charts**, **Optimize**, **Support**, **Settings**. Style with `--vscode-*` variables only. Tabs, buttons, inputs, and chips are ghost/pill controls: theme-mixed borders and top-shine gradients, not `--vscode-button-background`. Dark and light both derive from the IDE tokens (`body.vscode-dark` / `body.vscode-light`). User-chosen status colors come from settings (`okColor` / `warnColor`), not a hardcoded marketplace palette in CSS. Packaged defaults are mint/coral on dark themes and darker green/red on light themes.
- CSP nonce; `default-src 'none'`. No inline handlers without nonce.
- Host → webview: `{ type: 'data', events, spikeTokenThreshold, showSpikeWarning, showCriticalAlert, criticalTokenThreshold, criticalCostUsdThreshold, okColor, warnColor, historyLimit, historyFromDate, pollIntervalMinutes, showStatusBar, showToday, minimalMode, recentQueryCount, budgetDayBasis, optimizeDepth, language, i18n, burnRateGuard, burnRateWindowMinutes, burnRateWarningUsd, burnRateCriticalUsd, burnRateMinQueries, burnRateWarningToast, burnRateCriticalToast, burnRate, codeLinesInsight, codeLines, statusBarPreview, stats, charts, periods, mtd, optimize, support, refreshing }`. Webview → host: `{ type: 'ready' }` | `{ type: 'close' }` | `{ type: 'refresh' }` | `{ type: 'setSpikeThreshold', value }` | `{ type: 'setShowSpikeWarning', value }` | `{ type: 'setShowCriticalAlert', value }` | `{ type: 'setCriticalTokenThreshold', value }` | `{ type: 'setCriticalCostUsdThreshold', value }` | `{ type: 'setShowStatusBar', value }` | `{ type: 'setShowToday', value }` | `{ type: 'setMinimalMode', value }` | `{ type: 'setRecentQueryCount', value }` | `{ type: 'setPollIntervalMinutes', value }` | `{ type: 'setBudgetDayBasis', value }` | `{ type: 'setOptimizeDepth', value }` | `{ type: 'setLanguage', value }` | `{ type: 'setBurnRateGuard', value }` | `{ type: 'setBurnRateWindowMinutes', value }` | `{ type: 'setBurnRateWarningUsd', value }` | `{ type: 'setBurnRateCriticalUsd', value }` | `{ type: 'setBurnRateMinQueries', value }` | `{ type: 'setBurnRateWarningToast', value }` | `{ type: 'setBurnRateCriticalToast', value }` | `{ type: 'setCodeLinesInsight', value }` | `{ type: 'runOptimize' }` | `{ type: 'copyOptimizePrompt' }` | `{ type: 'openSupportLink', id }` | `{ type: 'setOkColor', value }` | `{ type: 'setWarnColor', value }` | `{ type: 'setHistoryLimit', value }` | `{ type: 'setHistoryFromDate', value }`. Apply Warn at immediately (input debounce + Apply). Show last (100–10,000, default 1000), From date (local `YYYY-MM-DD`; empty = Last N; Start of month / Today / Clear), Auto-refresh (1–60 min), Critical alert token/dollar thresholds, and Burn Rate Guard window/dollar/min-request values apply on Apply / Enter / blur — not while typing, because `min=100` on Show last rejects partial values like `2` when typing `2000`, and Critical alert `10000` k would otherwise save as `1` / `10` / `100`. Queries toolbar has **Over Warn at** (client filter for spike rows), **Refresh** (postMessage), plus Export CSV. Show last / From date live under Settings → Recent queries. Export CSV and Open Dashboard are allowlisted `command:` links (`cursorCost.exportCsv`, `cursorCost.openDashboard`).
- Settings tab holds every `cursorCost.*` key. First fieldset is **Language** (`cursorCost.language`: `en` default / `pl`) — panel, status bar, and toasts switch immediately. Status-bar settings live in one fieldset (sample preview, Show status bar, content, warnings, colors) with section titles inside the card. Together they include show status bar, Show Today, Minimal mode, and recent status-bar queries (1–10, default 3). The preview uses example spend, not live totals. **Critical alert** is a separate fieldset (blocking dialog at 10M tokens or $5 by default; independent of Show warnings). **Burn Rate Guard** fieldset (after Critical alert): enable, window minutes (2–60, default 10), warning $ (default 2), critical $ (default 5, never below warning), min requests (default 2), warning/critical toast toggles. Numbers save on Apply / Enter / blur. Dollar toasts need billed `costUsd`; included Pro $0 events still show token throughput on Statistics. **Generated lines** fieldset (after Burn Rate Guard): `cursorCost.codeLinesInsight` toggle for AI composer line totals vs git merged to main/master. **Monthly budget** fieldset: Pace by Working days (Mon–Fri, default) or All calendar days — applies to Today daily budget, MTD meters, and forecast charts. **Optimize** fieldset: Prompt depth Quick / Balanced (default) / Deep (`cursorCost.optimizeDepth`). Recent queries: Show last and **From date** (`cursorCost.historyFromDate`, local day; empty = Last N). Refresh: Auto-refresh interval. When Show warnings is off: no `!` and no green/red on the bar or table. Select/`option` lists use `--vscode-dropdown-*` (not input foreground alone) so dark themes stay readable.
- Statistics tab: Current/Today with progress meters, **Current burn rate** card when `burnRateGuard` is on (`$ / window`, optional `×` vs normal pace, Today $, meter vs critical $), banner when level is warning/critical, **Generated lines** as a fourth Status bar glossary card when `codeLinesInsight` is on (`AI · merged` hero, meters for AI lines and merged → default branch, ratio hint), **Monthly cost forecast** (plan-dependent: Team/Business/Enterprise → dollars + one Spend series; Pro → included % for Cursor Models / Other Models. Team: this month’s spend vs working days so far × daily budget. Pro: one meter per quota with cycle % + run-out date; 0–100% chart with used + forecast + ideal per quota; range toggle Today / 7 days / Month, default Month; forecast in warm dashed colors, ideal in cool dotted blues), cycle facts, Last N sample (total, average, median, cache hit, cost per 1M tokens, token mix, highlights), spend-by-model/kind bars. `burnRate` is `{ level, costUsd, tokens, queryCount, windowMinutes, multiplier, todayUsd, percent, summary, paceLabel, mixLabel, todayLabel, bannerTitle, bannerBody } | null`. `codeLines` is `{ enabled, source, disclaimer, summary, series, defaultBranch, workspacePath } | null`. Query rows include `inBurnWindow`; warn styling when warnings are on and level is warning/critical. No 7th column.
- Charts tab: two SVG **cumulative daily bar** series (tokens over time, cost over time — one bar per local calendar day from the Last N sample; height = running total up to that day), **AI lines vs merged** cumulative dual line when `codeLines` is present, plus the same **Monthly cost forecast** control as Statistics (meters, Today / 7 days / Month range, used/forecast/ideal). Oldest → newest. Forecast hover tip is one row per bar: `date label used / limit` (cumulative used vs that day’s budget allowance `forecast[].allowanceUsd`, not the spend-pace dashed line — today’s denominator stays put on refresh). Amounts are `--cost-warn` when used > limit, `--cost-ok` otherwise. Daily-chart tips stay title (day) + query count + that day’s tokens/cost + running total. Below: three cards (Today / This month / All time) with cost, cache hit, and input/output/cache mix. `charts` is `{ timestamp, time, tokens, costUsd, queryCount }[]`. `mtd` is `{ title, value, body, bars, metrics, overPace, verdict, unit, max, chart, forecast, series }` where `title` is `Monthly cost forecast`, `value` is unused (empty), `verdict` is `ok` | `tight` | `over`, `unit` is `usd` | `percent`, `max` caps the forecast axis (`100` for percent, `null` scales to data), `chart` is `{ date, weekday, workingDayIndex, dayUsedUsd, usedUsd, allowanceUsd }[]` (1st → today), `forecast` is the day backbone `{ date, weekday, workingDayIndex, allowanceUsd }[]` (1st → last day) and `series` is `{ id, label, day, used, forecast, ideal, runOutDate, runOutLabel }[]` — one line per included quota (Cursor Models, Other Models) or a single `Spend` line on dollar plans. Forecast chart bars use cumulative `used` (same scale as lines); `day` stays in tips for that-day burn. `day`/`used` are null after today, `forecast` spans the whole month, `ideal` is leftover ceiling from today to month end (`null` before today), `runOutDate` is the first day the forecast hits the ceiling (or null if it lasts), and values are dollars or included % when `unit` is `percent`. `periods` is the three cards. No cookies or access tokens.
- Optimize tab: `optimize` is `{ depth, summary, note, findings, prompt, prompts: { quick, balanced, deep }, estTokensSaved, estUsdSaved, hasProjection, lifetime: { totalTokens, totalUsd, summary, projects: { label, tokens, usd }[], empty }, empty }`. Three colored collapsible cards (Quick / Balanced / Deep) each with preview + Run; Default badge follows `optimizeDepth`. Toolbar **Run Optimize (Depth)** always pastes the **Default**-marked depth (`{ type: 'runOptimize', depth }` from the webview’s current default) into the last active Agent chat (`composer.focusComposer` first — not a new chat); card Run posts that card’s depth. `{ type: 'setOptimizeDepth', value }` / Set default on a card. Findings and prompts target the last red query (≥ Warn at). One collapsed `<details>` shows **Projected save per similar request** (`0 / 0.00 $` until `.ai/optimize-savings.md` (`cct-savings` with `project`, `tokens_mid`, `usd_mid`, `run`) exists; then mid tokens / USD from the agent). Expand for the note (projected cost on a similar request) and credited per-project rows (`globalState` mid deltas). Every Optimize prompt requires a closing report of mid tokens, mid USD, and project name. No chat transcript read by the extension.
- Support tab: thank-you copy + Buy Me a Coffee only. GitHub Sponsors markup is omitted from the webview until the account is live (`GITHUB_SPONSORS_URL` in `src/supportLinks.ts`). `support` is `{ buyMeACoffee: boolean, githubSponsors: boolean }`. `{ type: 'openSupportLink', id }` opens allowlisted external URLs via `env.openExternal`; empty Buy Me a Coffee URL stays Coming soon.
- Payload must not include cookies, access tokens, or emails beyond what PRD shows in the table.
- Keep JS small (vanilla). Do not add React.
